# Line endings.
#
# We develop on Windows and deploy on Linux, which is exactly the situation
# that produces a commit touching every file in the repository and changing
# nothing in any of them. It happened once: 39 files, 10,462 lines added,
# 10,462 removed, `git diff --ignore-all-space` completely empty.
#
# The fix is to let git decide. `text=auto` means git works out which files
# are text and stores those with LF no matter what the working tree looks
# like; `eol=lf` means they come back out as LF too, so the two never drift
# apart again. Nobody has to remember to configure anything.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and the Procfile run on Linux. A stray carriage return in a
# shebang gives you `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory`,
# which is a genuinely unpleasant hour of your life.
*.sh    text eol=lf
start.sh text eol=lf

# Binary. Git guesses right most of the time, but "most" is doing real work
# in that sentence and a mangled PNG is not obviously a line-ending problem.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.mp4   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.db    binary

# Keep the diff readable for the things a reviewer actually reads.
*.md    text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.py    text eol=lf diff=python
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.cff   text eol=lf
